[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the phrase at the center of your request: "tasks they care about."

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the app knows which tasks a user cares about.

  - [1mImplicit — a due date is the declaration of interest.[0m Setting a due date [3mis[0m saying "tell me about this one." No
    extra field, no extra UI, nothing to keep in sync. Tasks with no due date never notify.
  - [1mExplicit watch flag[0m — a star/bell toggle per task, independent of the due date. Lets you have a dated task you don't
    want pinged about. Costs a field, a toggle, and an empty-state question ("why am I getting nothing?" → because
    nothing is watched).
  - [1mRules[0m — notify on tasks matching criteria like priority or tag. Needs tags and priorities, which don't exist. This
    is a v3 feature for an app that doesn't have a v1.

  [1mMy recommendation: implicit.[0m "Watching" is a multi-user affordance — it exists so you can follow a task [3msomeone else[0m
  owns. Here you created every task yourself, so an explicit watch flag degrades into a second priority field that can
  silently contradict the due date. If the need for "dated but quiet" shows up in real use, adding a [38;5;153mmuted[39m flag later is
  a one-field change and the bus doesn't care. Going the other way — ripping out a watch system nobody used — is the
  expensive direction.
